1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,770
Microsoft has the source code
to Windows. They wrote the APIs.

2
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:07,610
They know exactly how it works inside.

3
00:00:08,850 --> 00:00:11,270
They have had thirty years,
and some of the largest

4
00:00:11,350 --> 00:00:13,960
engineering teams in
the history of software.

5
00:00:15,150 --> 00:00:16,460
And yet.

6
00:00:17,809 --> 00:00:21,219
Watch what a serious Windows
user does on a brand new machine.

7
00:00:22,419 --> 00:00:24,469
They install a different file manager.

8
00:00:24,599 --> 00:00:26,569
A different search tool.

9
00:00:27,119 --> 00:00:28,689
A different archive program.

10
00:00:29,623 --> 00:00:32,253
Image viewer. Video player. Music player.

11
00:00:33,132 --> 00:00:35,762
PDF reader. Text editor.

12
00:00:36,672 --> 00:00:37,982
And the list goes on...

13
00:00:39,032 --> 00:00:42,022
Before they do any actual
work, they rebuild the toolbox.

14
00:00:43,142 --> 00:00:44,672
So here is the question.

15
00:00:45,542 --> 00:00:49,192
Why doesn't Windows come with the tools
you need the moment the work gets harder?

16
00:00:52,302 --> 00:00:53,892
Start with the file manager.

17
00:00:54,932 --> 00:00:56,712
If you only want to open a folder and

18
00:00:56,792 --> 00:00:59,782
double-click a document,
File Explorer is fine.

19
00:01:01,092 --> 00:01:02,882
But if you need to do more with files...

20
00:01:04,322 --> 00:01:06,552
Compare two folders and sync them.

21
00:01:07,202 --> 00:01:09,592
Rename three hundred files by a pattern.

22
00:01:10,362 --> 00:01:13,092
See folder sizes without opening each one.

23
00:01:14,012 --> 00:01:18,002
And you discover a program that has
been doing all of that since 1993.

24
00:01:19,057 --> 00:01:23,267
Two panels. Keyboard shortcuts.
Synchronise. Compare. Rename. Archive.

25
00:01:24,231 --> 00:01:25,311
Written by one developer.

26
00:01:25,391 --> 00:01:27,941
In Switzerland. Called Christian Ghisler.

27
00:01:29,169 --> 00:01:31,859
And here is the part that says
more than the feature list.

28
00:01:32,979 --> 00:01:36,049
For the first nine years, it
was not called Total Commander.

29
00:01:37,339 --> 00:01:38,949
It was called Windows Commander.

30
00:01:40,099 --> 00:01:44,549
Then in the summer of 2002, a letter
arrived from a firm of lawyers.

31
00:01:45,909 --> 00:01:49,679
They represented, in their own words,
the owner of the trademark Windows.

32
00:01:51,019 --> 00:01:53,229
So Microsoft did not build that tool.

33
00:01:53,959 --> 00:01:56,049
Microsoft made it change its name.

34
00:01:58,659 --> 00:02:01,039
And before you decide that
nobody in Redmond ever

35
00:02:01,119 --> 00:02:04,149
had the idea of a proper
two-panel file manager...

36
00:02:05,829 --> 00:02:07,779
They did have it. They shipped it.

37
00:02:09,149 --> 00:02:10,329
DOS Shell, 1988.

38
00:02:10,409 --> 00:02:13,379
Left panel, right panel. In the box.

39
00:02:14,301 --> 00:02:16,031
Microsoft removed it.

40
00:02:18,581 --> 00:02:20,711
So the question sharpens immediately.

41
00:02:21,501 --> 00:02:23,531
This isn't a gap nobody noticed.

42
00:02:24,621 --> 00:02:27,951
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

43
00:02:28,721 --> 00:02:30,171
It really helps the channel.

44
00:02:31,511 --> 00:02:33,601
Keep going, because the pattern repeats.

45
00:02:34,801 --> 00:02:36,011
Finding a file.

46
00:02:36,761 --> 00:02:40,531
For years, the reliable answer was
a tiny program called Everything.

47
00:02:41,751 --> 00:02:43,101
You type three letters.

48
00:02:43,631 --> 00:02:45,181
The results are already there.

49
00:02:45,921 --> 00:02:50,511
No waiting. No wondering whether the
index has got round to your folder yet.

50
00:02:51,581 --> 00:02:55,051
And this one deserves a fair hearing,
because the trick is not magic.

51
00:02:56,171 --> 00:03:01,321
On an NTFS drive there is a master file
table, a list of everything on the disk.

52
00:03:02,368 --> 00:03:05,088
Everything reads that list
once, then follows the

53
00:03:05,168 --> 00:03:07,878
change journal the filesystem
is already keeping.

54
00:03:08,858 --> 00:03:10,568
That is why it feels instant.

55
00:03:11,748 --> 00:03:14,978
Windows Search is slower because
it is doing a harder job.

56
00:03:16,058 --> 00:03:20,228
It reads inside your documents, and it
respects who is allowed to see which file.

57
00:03:21,228 --> 00:03:22,938
So that one is not a failure.

58
00:03:23,728 --> 00:03:25,558
It's a different question being answered.

59
00:03:26,618 --> 00:03:28,928
Hold on to that, because
it is the exception.

60
00:03:31,778 --> 00:03:33,428
Where did the disk space go?

61
00:03:34,318 --> 00:03:37,868
Windows tells you that you have
used nine hundred gigabytes.

62
00:03:38,318 --> 00:03:39,518
Where?

63
00:03:39,950 --> 00:03:42,900
And you end up installing a
program that draws you a map.

64
00:03:44,030 --> 00:03:45,680
And what about copying files?

65
00:03:46,710 --> 00:03:50,660
Historically, if one file failed,
Explorer cancelled the whole copy.

66
00:03:51,780 --> 00:03:53,190
Which files made it?

67
00:03:53,920 --> 00:03:57,090
Where do I pick it up?
Can I skip just that one?

68
00:03:57,910 --> 00:03:59,730
So people installed
something that would skip

69
00:03:59,810 --> 00:04:02,320
it, carry on, and tell
them what it skipped.

70
00:04:03,600 --> 00:04:05,470
Deleting has a different mystery.

71
00:04:06,260 --> 00:04:08,370
That one has a whole video of its own.

72
00:04:09,290 --> 00:04:13,040
What about archives?
Windows opens a zip file.

73
00:04:13,825 --> 00:04:16,355
But there is a difference
between supporting a format...

74
00:04:17,815 --> 00:04:20,005
...and giving someone a good tool for it.

75
00:04:20,895 --> 00:04:24,245
Screenshots. Print screen,
open Paint, paste, crop.

76
00:04:25,205 --> 00:04:27,515
You had to do that for about twenty years.

77
00:04:28,615 --> 00:04:32,065
And uninstalling software is
where it gets genuinely strange.

78
00:04:33,325 --> 00:04:35,285
Programs left files behind.

79
00:04:35,365 --> 00:04:37,325
Registry entries.

80
00:04:37,405 --> 00:04:39,795
Folders nobody would ever find.

81
00:04:40,545 --> 00:04:42,275
So people installed better uninstallers.

82
00:04:44,012 --> 00:04:47,502
But some of those uninstallers
had a stranger job than that.

83
00:04:48,572 --> 00:04:49,772
They watched.

84
00:04:50,716 --> 00:04:54,706
You ran them first, then you ran the
installer, and they recorded what changed.

85
00:04:55,976 --> 00:04:57,386
Think about what that means.

86
00:04:58,966 --> 00:05:02,576
You installed a program to watch
another program install itself.

87
00:05:03,726 --> 00:05:06,536
Because Windows itself
did not necessarily know.

88
00:05:09,586 --> 00:05:11,616
Which brings us to the sharpest case.

89
00:05:12,716 --> 00:05:13,916
Not responding.

90
00:05:14,606 --> 00:05:17,216
Task Manager knows. It
says so on the screen.

91
00:05:18,296 --> 00:05:21,386
But if you want to know why,
you install Process Explorer.

92
00:05:22,176 --> 00:05:23,376
Threads.

93
00:05:23,500 --> 00:05:24,700
Handles.

94
00:05:24,910 --> 00:05:27,080
Which file the program is waiting on.

95
00:05:27,570 --> 00:05:29,320
Which process started it.

96
00:05:30,090 --> 00:05:32,880
And Process Explorer came from
a company called Sysinternals.

97
00:05:34,453 --> 00:05:35,963
Microsoft bought them.

98
00:05:36,743 --> 00:05:37,943
That was in 2006.

99
00:05:39,431 --> 00:05:41,241
And they got the man who wrote it.

100
00:05:42,191 --> 00:05:46,221
Mark Russinovich. Today,
he is the CTO of Azure.

101
00:05:47,181 --> 00:05:51,231
Microsoft has owned that tool, and the
person who wrote it, for twenty years.

102
00:05:52,391 --> 00:05:54,101
They host the download themselves.

103
00:05:55,431 --> 00:05:57,181
And it is still not in Windows.

104
00:06:00,241 --> 00:06:03,111
So the answer cannot be
that Microsoft couldn't.

105
00:06:03,851 --> 00:06:06,881
Which means the real question
was never why they failed.

106
00:06:07,991 --> 00:06:09,741
It's why they chose not to.

107
00:06:12,771 --> 00:06:14,061
There are three explanations.

108
00:06:15,223 --> 00:06:19,213
Only one of them has court
records. Keep an eye on which.

109
00:06:20,057 --> 00:06:22,587
The first is the one
everybody already knows.

110
00:06:23,747 --> 00:06:25,657
Windows was sold as a platform.

111
00:06:26,757 --> 00:06:30,587
Microsoft did not want to own
every application on your computer.

112
00:06:31,217 --> 00:06:33,567
They wanted to own the
floor it all stood on.

113
00:06:34,517 --> 00:06:37,397
Need a file manager?
Somebody will build one.

114
00:06:37,477 --> 00:06:38,417
Need a disk tool?

115
00:06:38,497 --> 00:06:40,747
Somebody will build one.

116
00:06:41,437 --> 00:06:43,207
And that worked spectacularly.

117
00:06:43,740 --> 00:06:46,460
But on its own it explains
nothing, because Apple sells

118
00:06:46,540 --> 00:06:49,510
a platform too, and Apple
ships a screenshot tool.

119
00:06:52,210 --> 00:06:55,380
So here is the second reason,
and this one has court dates.

120
00:06:56,620 --> 00:07:00,850
In 1998, the American government
took Microsoft to court.

121
00:07:02,080 --> 00:07:03,830
Not for writing a bad browser.

122
00:07:04,990 --> 00:07:06,420
For putting it in Windows.

123
00:07:08,770 --> 00:07:11,420
A judge ordered the
company to be split in two.

124
00:07:12,430 --> 00:07:16,900
That order was thrown out on appeal,
and the case settled in 2001.

125
00:07:17,970 --> 00:07:20,430
And the question underneath
it, whether putting the browser

126
00:07:20,510 --> 00:07:24,280
in Windows was legal at all,
was never actually answered.

127
00:07:27,320 --> 00:07:32,290
Then in 2004, the European Commission
fined them nearly half a billion euros.

128
00:07:33,710 --> 00:07:37,760
And ordered them to ship a version of
Windows with the media player taken out.

129
00:07:38,920 --> 00:07:40,590
Look at that sentence again.

130
00:07:41,530 --> 00:07:45,180
A regulator required Microsoft
to remove a tool from Windows.

131
00:07:48,290 --> 00:07:50,200
And this is not ancient history.

132
00:07:51,280 --> 00:07:55,340
Starting in 2016, an antivirus
company complained to

133
00:07:55,420 --> 00:07:59,130
regulators in Russia, then
in Germany, then in Brussels.

134
00:08:00,600 --> 00:08:03,170
Because Windows had
started defending itself.

135
00:08:04,310 --> 00:08:09,820
They withdrew the complaints in 2017, once
Microsoft agreed to change how it behaved.

136
00:08:11,830 --> 00:08:13,880
So put yourself in that meeting room.

137
00:08:14,920 --> 00:08:17,200
Somebody proposes shipping
the best archive tool

138
00:08:17,280 --> 00:08:20,230
on the market, for free,
to a billion machines.

139
00:08:21,380 --> 00:08:23,530
There is a market for archive tools.

140
00:08:24,540 --> 00:08:28,810
Shipping the best possible tool is the
most expensive thing Microsoft can do.

141
00:08:31,860 --> 00:08:33,230
And only for Microsoft.

142
00:08:34,100 --> 00:08:37,750
A smaller company could ship
exactly the same tool and be fine.

143
00:08:38,950 --> 00:08:43,340
Once you are dominant, competition law
limits how you can use that position.

144
00:08:44,250 --> 00:08:46,670
And replacing the very companies
that built their business

145
00:08:46,750 --> 00:08:50,160
on your platform can start to
look a lot like abusing it.

146
00:08:51,230 --> 00:08:53,620
Microsoft was trapped by its own success.

147
00:08:54,890 --> 00:08:57,260
And the third reason is the quietest one.

148
00:08:58,250 --> 00:09:02,040
Putting something in Windows means
supporting it for the next twenty years.

149
00:09:03,190 --> 00:09:06,040
It has to keep working.
It can never be removed.

150
00:09:06,650 --> 00:09:09,320
It becomes somebody's
whole workflow by Thursday.

151
00:09:10,590 --> 00:09:14,580
Which is why, in 1996, Microsoft
did something revealing.

152
00:09:15,800 --> 00:09:18,870
They released a set of their own
Windows utilities called PowerToys.

153
00:09:20,440 --> 00:09:23,780
Written by Microsoft engineers.
Downloaded separately.

154
00:09:23,860 --> 00:09:25,950
And officially unsupported.

155
00:09:27,130 --> 00:09:28,780
We made these. They're good.

156
00:09:29,250 --> 00:09:30,820
We are not putting them in the box.

157
00:09:33,550 --> 00:09:36,520
And that same rule explains
the biggest gap of all.

158
00:09:37,510 --> 00:09:39,690
Twenty programs. Twenty updaters.

159
00:09:39,770 --> 00:09:41,540
One checks at startup.

160
00:09:41,770 --> 00:09:43,900
One installs a service.

161
00:09:44,870 --> 00:09:47,050
You log in on a Monday
and six of them want

162
00:09:47,130 --> 00:09:49,640
your attention before
you have opened anything.

163
00:09:50,770 --> 00:09:52,800
Linux solved this decades ago.

164
00:09:53,610 --> 00:09:55,420
One command updates everything.

165
00:09:56,360 --> 00:09:57,670
So why couldn't Windows?

166
00:09:58,463 --> 00:10:01,433
Because that command works
for a reason people forget.

167
00:10:02,573 --> 00:10:05,503
A Linux distribution
builds the software itself.

168
00:10:06,273 --> 00:10:09,463
It compiles it, packages
it, and knows what is in it.

169
00:10:10,323 --> 00:10:12,553
Microsoft does not build your software.

170
00:10:13,583 --> 00:10:15,373
Windows is not a distribution.

171
00:10:16,383 --> 00:10:18,853
It is a floor that anyone
may put anything on.

172
00:10:19,923 --> 00:10:23,713
Which is exactly why that
installer-watching program existed.

173
00:10:24,883 --> 00:10:27,173
Nobody had the list. Not even Windows.

174
00:10:30,273 --> 00:10:31,683
So that is the answer.

175
00:10:32,693 --> 00:10:35,563
Platform, lawsuits, and the weight
of anything you put in the box.

176
00:10:38,363 --> 00:10:39,993
Except something has changed.

177
00:10:41,843 --> 00:10:44,153
Because Windows now has a package manager.

178
00:10:45,203 --> 00:10:46,993
It arrived in 2021.

179
00:10:47,843 --> 00:10:50,033
One command, and your programs update.

180
00:10:50,913 --> 00:10:53,953
Windows has a proper
terminal now. It runs Linux.

181
00:10:54,033 --> 00:10:58,523
PowerToys came back in 2019, open source.

182
00:10:59,413 --> 00:11:00,613
So why now?

183
00:11:01,245 --> 00:11:03,935
Partly because the thing
being protected has moved.

184
00:11:05,135 --> 00:11:08,749
Windows is not where Microsoft
makes its money any more.

185
00:11:09,199 --> 00:11:12,289
It now sits in the smallest of
the company's three divisions.

186
00:11:13,539 --> 00:11:16,409
That division brings in less
than a fifth of the revenue.

187
00:11:17,199 --> 00:11:18,869
It also contains the Xbox.

188
00:11:20,179 --> 00:11:22,949
And nobody is going to court
over a screenshot tool.

189
00:11:23,989 --> 00:11:27,719
The fight moved to browsers, then
to phones, then to the data centre.

190
00:11:29,035 --> 00:11:31,785
The desktop is no longer
the thing being defended.

191
00:11:33,795 --> 00:11:35,545
But look closely at what shipped.

192
00:11:37,014 --> 00:11:38,424
PowerToys, you download yourself.

193
00:11:39,134 --> 00:11:41,114
Process Explorer, you download yourself.

194
00:11:41,194 --> 00:11:44,184
Sysinternals, you download yourself.

195
00:11:45,144 --> 00:11:50,034
Thirty years later, Microsoft still keeps
its best tools one step outside Windows.

196
00:11:53,524 --> 00:11:55,394
So the strange result stands.

197
00:11:56,434 --> 00:11:58,934
Microsoft built a platform
so successful that

198
00:11:59,014 --> 00:12:01,584
other people built
everything missing from it.

199
00:12:02,644 --> 00:12:05,564
And because those tools
existed, Microsoft never had to.

200
00:12:05,644 --> 00:12:09,494
And for years, doing too
much carried legal risk.

201
00:12:10,485 --> 00:12:12,605
Windows turned out to
be extraordinarily good

202
00:12:12,685 --> 00:12:15,215
at hosting the tools it
never shipped itself.

203
00:12:18,075 --> 00:12:24,105
The user paid for that in complexity.
A better file manager. A better search.

204
00:12:24,855 --> 00:12:26,745
Every one of them updating separately.

205
00:12:27,865 --> 00:12:32,035
Which means the real Windows experience
was never install it and you're done.

206
00:12:33,255 --> 00:12:34,945
It was: install Windows.

207
00:12:35,702 --> 00:12:38,892
Then build your own operating
environment around it.

208
00:12:39,812 --> 00:12:43,222
And the strangest part isn't
that Microsoft couldn't do it.

209
00:12:43,872 --> 00:12:46,372
It's that for twenty years,
being brilliant at this

210
00:12:46,452 --> 00:12:48,802
was the one thing Windows
could not afford to be.
